<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Revolutionary Serbia</h1><p class="st-meta">1804 CE – 1813 CE · capital Belgrade</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Insurgent Serbian state of the First Serbian Uprising, which drove out Ottoman janissaries and laid the foundations of modern Serbia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1801 renegade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a>, the dahije, seized power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjak-of-smederevo">Pashalik of Belgrade</a> and terrorised the Serbian population. After they murdered dozens of Serbian notables, an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orasac">Orašac</a> in February 1804 chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadorde">Karađorđe Petrović</a> to lead a revolt. The uprising soon turned against Ottoman rule itself, winning battles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ivankovac">Ivankovac</a> in 1805 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-misar">Mišar</a> in 1806.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The insurgents took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1806 and created a Governing Council in 1805, alongside assemblies of local leaders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadorde">Karađorđe's</a> growing personal power as supreme leader. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> supported them during its war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. The scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dositej-obradovic">Dositej Obradović</a> became the first minister of education, and a Great School opened in Belgrade in 1808.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> made peace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bucharest-1812">Bucharest</a> in 1812 to face <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> reconquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> in 1813 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadorde">Karađorđe</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-serbian-uprising">Second Serbian Uprising</a> of 1815 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milos-obrenovic">Miloš Obrenović</a> led to the autonomous <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-serbia">Principality of Serbia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/revolutionary-serbia.jpg" alt="Revolutionary Serbia" width="320" height="325"><figcaption>Image: TRAJAN 117 This W3C-unspecified vector image was created with Inkscape . · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Territory_of_the_Serbian_Uprising_(1812).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1804 CE – 1813 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Belgrade</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Karađorđe Petrović</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Serbian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karađorđe's Serbia, Governing Council of Serbia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1808&amp;focus=revolutionary-serbia" aria-label="Revolutionary Serbia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5168 594 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Revolutionary Serbia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5488 850L5709 970M5488 850L6487 457M5488 850L5501 875"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M6487 457h0M5501 875h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5488 850h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1808&amp;focus=revolutionary-serbia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
